# Line endings.
#
# This repository stores LF and, until this file existed, nothing said so: there
# was no .gitattributes and no core.autocrlf, so a Windows checkout wrote CRLF
# into the working tree while git kept LF in the objects. Every file then read as
# modified, and a one-line edit staged as a whole-file rewrite - a 4-line rename
# in PrinterReactor.java came out as 185 insertions and 185 deletions.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text and normalise it to LF on the way in;
# `eol=lf` writes LF back out, on every platform, so the working tree and the
# objects agree and the churn cannot come back.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two exceptions, and they are genuine: cmd.exe requires CRLF in a batch
# file and mis-parses one without it, so these must NOT follow the rule above.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The POSIX wrapper is the mirror image: it is run by /bin/sh, and a CR at the
# end of its shebang makes the interpreter unfindable ("required file not
# found"). It is already covered by the default, but it is spelled out because
# getting it wrong breaks the build on Linux and in CI.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Binary. `text=auto` would almost certainly detect these anyway; declaring them
# means "almost" never has to be right, and it keeps them out of text diffs.
*.jar   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.otf   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.png   binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary

# Generated, and large. Collapsed in diffs and excluded from GitHub's language
